southwest medical hospice volunteers  continue to provide comfort for our  community when it's needed the most  as a thank you let's honor this month's  hospice hero  jessica take a look  my name is jessica corvis i'm a hospice  volunteer with southwest medical  associates  i'm here at sunset park because i think  it's one of the  more beautiful places in las vegas and  it was also where i chose to bring  my lab when we  when she was on dog hospice and we were  getting ready to say goodbye to her  we brought her here and so it's a really  special place  for me personally sometimes i come here  after  i've seen patients and i just need some  quiet time  in a peaceful place and i look i listen  to the birds and i  watch the ducks and it's just a very  calming environment  when i got out of the navy the job that  i had didn't translate very well to a  civilian position  so i got a job as a cna and  i worked with some really great nurses  in that field and i really enjoyed  that work so when i was working in  nursing i  typically did alzheimer's care long-term  care end-of-life care  i didn't work for hospice specifically  when i was in nursing  so i i had been around it but i hadn't  experienced it for myself  when covid happened i was at home on  facebook like everyone  and i saw the ad to volunteer for  southwest medical associates  and i filled out the interest form which  took about a minute  from there there's some things you have  to do to prepare to actually volunteer  some training some background checks and  things and once i completed those things  i started volunteering my  typical contribution is is with the  patient  directly but there are a lot of services  that hospice offers  it can be doing things for the family so  that they can be with the patients  instead  there's a lot of services after they've  already gone through their loss  that they can take advantage of support  wise later  and then there's a lot of administrative  things that people can do so there's a  very wide spectrum of services that  hospice does offer  and i think that sometimes people just  associate it with  an ending and a bad time and certainly  it is  happening at a bad time but i also think  that  what hospice brings is that they make  that bad time or that sad time  easier better more comfortable kinder  i've definitely enjoyed the experience  i think that there's again something  that  anyone can find a place if they're  interested in doing it  for just a few hours a week or a few  hours a month they can participate in  that  it can be as close to dealing with the  patient or is removed from that as  they're comfortable with i'm really  proud to be a hospice hero with  southwest medical associates  i think they do a great service for the  community and  they help people at their most  vulnerable moments  congrats and thank you jessica for more  information on southwest medical hospice  or to become a volunteer yourself  call 702-671-1111 or visit the website  on your screen  remember there are virtual and in-person  opportunities as a hospice volunteer  we're going to be right back with  breaking news and an update on the  military plane crash
 

